   <div class="section-divider"><span class="section-divider-label">Point of View</span></div>
   <!-- POINT OF VIEW -->
<section id="point-of-view" class="mb-28 reveal">
  <div>
    <span class="text-amber-400 font-mono text-sm uppercase tracking-wider">POV Articles</span>
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-2">Three Arguments Worth Making.</h2>
    <p class="text-neutral-400 max-w-2xl mt-3">Not conference slides. Not LinkedIn platitudes. Arguments built from 15 years in the data layer - where AI systems actually break.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="bg-neutral-900/30 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4">
        <p class="text-white text-sm font-medium leading-snug">"AI tools are not your strategy. They are your exposure - until the data layer underneath them is governed."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900/30 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4">
        <p class="text-white text-sm font-medium leading-snug">"Most AI governance frameworks are written after the risk shows up. By then, you're managing the incident, not the architecture."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-neutral-900/30 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4">
        <p class="text-white text-sm font-medium leading-snug">"If your AI system cannot be audited end-to-end, it is not enterprise-ready. It's a prototype with production traffic."</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 space-y-6" id="pov-articles">

    <!-- Article 1 -->
    <div class="bg-neutral-900/40 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-6 md:p-8">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <div>
            <span class="text-amber-400 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest">The Flagship Argument</span>
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold mt-2">AI Doesn't Fail at Models. It Fails at Systems.</h3>
            <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm mt-2 max-w-2xl">Most AI failures aren't model failures. They are system failures. The model gets blamed because it's the visible output. The real failure almost always sits upstream in the infrastructure that feeds it.</p>
          </div>
          <button onclick="toggleArticle('article1')" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="article1" class="flex-shrink-0 text-sm font-mono text-amber-400 border border-amber-500/30 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-amber-500/10 transition" id="article1-btn">Read Article ↓</button>
        </div>
        <div id="article1" class="mt-6 hidden">
          <div class="space-y-4 border-t border-white/10 pt-6">
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Models don't operate in a vacuum. They are downstream artifacts of pipelines, contracts, and assumptions. When those things break, the model doesn't "fail" in any meaningful sense. Instead, it behaves exactly as the system allows it to.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">The Downstream Trap</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Think about data pipelines. In theory, they are deterministic. In practice, they tend to degrade silently. A schema change in a source system might drop a column, or a timestamp might shift from UTC to local time. Perhaps a late-arriving dataset gets truncated by a job with a hard cutoff. Nothing crashes. Dashboards still render and the model still scores, but the signal has shifted. No one notices until the real-world outcomes begin to drift.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">When Systems Stop Agreeing</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">I've seen production models trained on one definition of an "active customer" while the data being served used another. This didn't happen because of a bad modeling decision. It happened because two pipelines, owned by different teams, evolved independently. The model didn't fail; the system simply stopped agreeing with itself.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Then we have integration seams - the most fragile parts of any AI system. Every handoff is a compression point where context is lost. Features get re-encoded, null handling changes, and business logic gets reimplemented just slightly differently. By the time data reaches the model, it's often a distorted version of what was originally intended. Yet most teams still treat these seams as simple plumbing rather than critical control surfaces.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">The Missing Loop</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">The biggest gap is feedback. Very few organizations engineer a closed loop where outputs are validated against real-world outcomes and traced back to specific data conditions. Instead, they monitor accuracy in isolation. When performance drops, they retrain or swap architectures. All the while, the underlying issue - a broken join, a stale feature store, or a misaligned aggregation window - remains untouched.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">The Reality of System Integrity</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">We have reached a point where precision at the model layer is a lie if the system feeding it is a black box. You aren't managing an AI; you are managing a high-speed engine with a failing fuel pump. Stop tuning the engine and fix the intake.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed font-medium">Production AI isn't about model sophistication. It's about system integrity. If your architecture allows for silent drift, your AI is already compromised. Not eventually. Right now.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Article 2 -->
    <div class="bg-neutral-900/40 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-6 md:p-8">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <div>
            <span class="text-blue-400 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest">The Execution Argument</span>
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold mt-2">The "Almost Ready" Trap: Why AI Platforms Stall</h3>
            <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm mt-2 max-w-2xl">Most AI platforms don't fail in production. They stall long before they ever get there. The stall happens in the data layer, and it happens in ways that were never properly scoped.</p>
          </div>
          <button onclick="toggleArticle('article2')" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="article2" class="flex-shrink-0 text-sm font-mono text-blue-400 border border-blue-500/30 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-500/10 transition" id="article2-btn">Read Article ↓</button>
        </div>
        <div id="article2" class="mt-6 hidden">
          <div class="space-y-4 border-t border-white/10 pt-6">
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">From the outside, it looks like a modeling problem. Teams assume the algorithms need improvement, the features need tuning, or the use case needs refinement. But from where I sit on the delivery and data platform side, the pattern is consistent. The stall happens in the data layer.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">1. Integration Is a System, Not a Task</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Early plans assume data integration is straightforward: identify sources, map fields, and move data. In practice, integration is where legacy systems and modern ambitions collide. During my time at Accenture, I saw how often "connecting three systems" was underestimated. Source systems rarely agree on definitions, identifiers don't reconcile cleanly, and latency varies wildly. Every integration point becomes a negotiation between systems that were never designed to speak the same language. What was scoped as a simple connection becomes a multi-month effort in identity resolution and schema alignment. None of this is visible in early demos because those run on curated datasets. The platform doesn't fail here; it just never stabilizes enough to move forward.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">2. The Collapse of Data Quality Under Load</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">In pre-production, data looks clean because it is sampled, filtered, and often manually corrected. But production introduces volume, variability, and endless edge cases that no sandbox can replicate. Validation rules that passed in testing are often bypassed for the sake of a "performance win." You aren't optimizing when you do this; you are mortgaging your future reliability. I've seen feature pipelines that produced stable outputs in staging become unusable in production within weeks. Once a team stops trusting the data, deployment stops immediately.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">3. The Missing Reality Loop</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Most AI platforms are built as one-way streets. They ingest data, generate predictions, and deliver outputs. What's missing is a structured feedback loop that answers a simple question: was the system right? I've seen platforms where the only feedback loop was a quarterly review meeting where stakeholders complained about inaccurate outputs from three months prior. By then, the trail is cold. Errors aren't traceable and drift isn't diagnosable.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed font-medium">Most platforms don't fail. They sit in a permanent state of "almost ready" until funding or executive patience runs out. If you don't have a closed loop, you don't have a production platform. You have a prototype that hasn't met reality yet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Article 3 -->
    <div class="bg-neutral-900/40 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="p-6 md:p-8">
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <div>
            <span class="text-teal-400 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest">The Control Argument</span>
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold mt-2">AI Governance is Theater (Unless it's Architecture)</h3>
            <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm mt-2 max-w-2xl">Most enterprise AI governance conversations are happening at the wrong layer. You cannot govern an AI system without first governing the data that feeds it. Most enterprises haven't done that work yet.</p>
          </div>
          <button onclick="toggleArticle('article3')" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="article3" class="flex-shrink-0 text-sm font-mono text-teal-400 border border-teal-500/30 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-teal-500/10 transition" id="article3-btn">Read Article ↓</button>
        </div>
        <div id="article3" class="mt-6 hidden">
          <div class="space-y-4 border-t border-white/10 pt-6">
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Organizations are investing heavily in model risk frameworks and review boards, but these programs are often theater because they operate downstream of the real risk.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Lessons from High-Stakes Environments</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Before focusing on AI, I spent years in federal healthcare and national data exchange environments where data quality was never theoretical. At the Department of Veterans Affairs, we managed records tied directly to care delivery and benefits decisions. In that world, a duplicate patient record wasn't just a reporting error. It could delay care, misroute a referral, or create dangerous gaps in a veteran's treatment history.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">I remember a case where fragmented records across systems led to inconsistent views of a patient's status. Nothing was "broken" in isolation. Each system held its own version of the truth. However, without strong identity resolution and data contracts, the aggregate view was simply wrong. Downstream systems then made decisions based on that inconsistency.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">I've watched that same pattern play out in every AI initiative since. We treat governance as a program - a set of policies, stewardship roles, and review boards - rather than as an engineered layer of the platform itself.</p>
            <h4 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Governance as Architecture</h4>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">Governance at scale cannot work as a manual process. It has to work as architecture. That means enforceable data contracts at system boundaries, not informal agreements. It means technical lineage that is traceable in the code, not something reconstructed by hand during an audit. It means schema validation, identity resolution, and quality checks embedded directly in the pipelines. It also means that when data deviates, the system must fail loudly.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed">If you are sitting in a review board meeting discussing "bias" while your data pipelines are silently diverging, you aren't governing. You are performing. Real governance isn't a checklist; it's an engineering constraint.</p>
            <p class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed font-medium">Leaders who trust a model without evidence-based data contracts aren't leading. They are gambling.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

